Yes, both are known MSTest v4 breaking changes. Here's the breakdown:

---

### 1. Lost test history in Azure DevOps

MSTest v4 changed the default **test-ID generation strategy**. New IDs mean ADO treats every test as brand-new. Fix by adding a `.runsettings` file that pins the old strategy:

```xml
<RunSettings>
  <MSTest>
    <TestIdGenerationStrategy>DisplayName</TestIdGenerationStrategy>
  </MSTest>
</RunSettings>
```

Then reference it in your csproj or CI pipeline (`dotnet test --settings my.runsettings`).

---

### 2. `TestContext.TestName` in `[ClassInitialize]`

In v4, the `TestContext` passed to `[ClassInitialize]` is now a **class-level context** — it isn't associated with any specific test. Accessing `TestName` (or other test-scoped properties) throws because no test is running yet. In v3 it silently returned the name of whichever test triggered initialization first (unreliable anyway).

**Fix**: remove the `TestName` access from `ClassInit`, or move that logic into `[TestInitialize]` where a per-test context is available:

```csharp
[TestClass]
public class SetupTests
{
    public TestContext TestContext { get; set; }

    [ClassInitialize]
    public static void ClassInit(TestContext context)
    {
        // Only use class-level context here (e.g., configuration, shared resources)
    }

    [TestInitialize]
    public void TestInit()
    {
        var testName = TestContext.TestName; // safe here
        // Log setup for specific test
    }

    [TestMethod]
    public void RunTest()
    {
        Assert.IsTrue(true);
    }
}
```

---

### 3. Other v4 pitfalls to watch for

| Area | Change |
|---|---|
| **DataRow display** | `[DataRow]` tests may generate different display names, also affecting ADO history |
| **Parallel execution** | Defaults may differ; review `[Parallelize]` settings |
| **Assert precision** | Some `Assert.AreEqual` overloads for floating-point tightened precision handling |
| **Timeout behavior** | `[Timeout]` now uses `CancellationToken` cooperative cancellation instead of thread-abort |
| **ClassCleanup timing** | Default changed to run at end of class (not end of assembly) |

